::ALG2_U04_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence has a constant multiplicative ratio? {
= 3, 6, 12, 24#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 2, so it is exponential.
~ 3, 6, 9, 12#This has a constant difference of 3.
~ 20, 17, 14, 11#This has a constant difference of -3.
~ 1, 4, 7, 10#This has a constant difference of 3.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>In f(x) = 12(0.5)<sup>x</sup>, what is the base? {
~ 12#This is the initial value, not the base.
= 0.5#Correct. The base is the repeated multiplication factor inside the exponent.
~ 6#This is f(1), not the base.
~ x#The exponent is the input variable, not the base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>A table has f(0)=7, f(1)=21, f(2)=63. Which model fits? {
~ f(x) = 3(7)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the initial value and base.
~ f(x) = 7 + 14x#The differences do not remain constant, so a linear model does not fit.
= f(x) = 7(3)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 7 and each output is multiplied by 3.
~ f(x) = 21(3)<sup>x</sup>#This uses f(1) as the initial value.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 4(5)<sup>x</sup>, what is f(2)? {
~ 40#This uses 5 times 2 instead of 5 squared.
~ 25#This ignores the initial value 4.
~ 500#This multiplies by an extra factor of 5.
= 100#Correct. Compute 4 times 25 to get 100.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does the 9 represent in A(t) = 9(2)<sup>t</sup>? {
= Initial amount#Correct. At t = 0, A(0) = 9, so it is the starting value.
~ Growth factor#The growth factor is 2.
~ Time interval#The variable t represents time.
~ Final amount#The final amount depends on the value of t.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>A quantity starts at 10 and is multiplied by 4 each step. Which function models it? {
~ g(x) = 4(10)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the starting value and multiplier.
= g(x) = 10(4)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The starting value is 10 and the repeated multiplier is 4.
~ g(x) = 10 + 4x#This uses repeated addition instead of multiplication.
~ g(x) = 40x#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which point is always on f(x) = a(b)<sup>x</sup>? {
~ (0, b)#The base is not the output at x = 0 unless a = b.
~ (1, a)#At x = 1, the output is ab.
= (0, a)#Correct. Since b<sup>0</sup> = 1, f(0) = a.
~ (b, a)#This confuses the base with an input value.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>If h(x) = 3(2)<sup>x</sup>, what is h(4)? {
~ 24#This uses 2 cubed instead of 2 to the fourth power.
~ 16#This ignores the factor 3.
~ 81#This incorrectly raises 3 to the fourth power.
= 48#Correct. Compute 3 times 16 to get 48.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>A graph crosses the y-axis at 15 and multiplies by 1.2 each step. Which model fits? {
= f(x) = 15(1.2)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The y-intercept is the initial value and 1.2 is the repeated factor.
~ f(x) = 1.2(15)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the initial value and base.
~ f(x) = 15 + 1.2x#This is linear.
~ f(x) = 16.2<sup>x</sup>#This combines the parameters incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement describes f(x) = 2(3)<sup>x</sup>? {
~ The output increases by 3 each step.#That would describe a linear pattern.
= The output triples when x increases by 1.#Correct. The base 3 means each step multiplies the output by 3.
~ The initial value is 3.#The initial value is 2.
~ The output is always 6.#The output changes as x changes.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which model has initial value 8 and base 5? {
~ y = 5(8)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the initial value and base.
~ y = 8 + 5x#This is linear.
= y = 8(5)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The coefficient 8 is the initial value and 5 is the base.
~ y = 40<sup>x</sup>#This combines the values incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is the common ratio of 2, 14, 98, 686? {
~ 12#This is the first difference, not the ratio.
~ 14#This is the second term, not the common ratio.
~ 49#This is 7 squared, not the step ratio.
= 7#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 7.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which context is exponential? {
= A culture triples every day.#Correct. Tripling each day is repeated multiplication.
~ A tank loses 5 gallons each hour.#Losing a fixed amount is linear.
~ A runner adds 2 miles each week.#Adding a fixed amount is linear.
~ A fee increases by 4 dollars each month.#Adding a fixed dollar amount is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>If f(0)=11 and the base is 2, which function fits? {
~ f(x) = 2(11)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the parameters.
= f(x) = 11(2)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 11 and the repeated factor is 2.
~ f(x) = 11 + 2x#This is linear.
~ f(x) = 22x#This is proportional, not exponential.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x)=5(2)<sup>x</sup>, what is the ratio f(3)/f(2)? {
~ 5#This is the initial value.
~ 10#This is f(1), not the ratio.
= 2#Correct. Consecutive outputs are multiplied by the base, which is 2.
~ 20#This is f(2), not the ratio between consecutive terms.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression represents three repeated doublings of 9? {
~ 9 + 2 + 2 + 2#This uses repeated addition.
~ 2(9)<sup>3</sup>#This raises the initial value instead of using repeated doubling.
~ 9(3)<sup>2</sup>#This uses the wrong repeated factor.
= 9(2)<sup>3</sup>#Correct. Three doublings multiply 9 by 2 three times.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is f(1) for f(x)=a(b)<sup>x</sup>? {
= ab#Correct. Substituting x = 1 gives a times b.
~ a#That is f(0).
~ b#This ignores the initial factor a.
~ a + b#The model multiplies, not adds.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>A table has outputs 100, 50, 25, 12.5. What is the base? {
~ 2#That is the reciprocal of the factor.
= 0.5#Correct. Each output is half the previous output.
~ 50#This is the second output, not the ratio.
~ -50#The output decreases by multiplying by 0.5, not subtracting 50 every time.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which function has y-intercept 6? {
~ f(x) = 3(6)<sup>x</sup>#This has y-intercept 3.
~ f(x) = 6x + 3#This is not exponential.
= f(x) = 6(3)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. The y-intercept is f(0), which equals the coefficient 6.
~ f(x) = 18(3)<sup>x</sup>#This has y-intercept 18.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which table fits f(x)=2(4)<sup>x</sup> for x=0,1,2? {
~ 4, 8, 12#This is linear.
~ 2, 6, 10#This adds 4 each time.
~ 8, 32, 128#This starts at f(1), not f(0).
= 2, 8, 32#Correct. Evaluate 2(4)<sup>x</sup> at x = 0, 1, 2.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does b represent in f(x)=a(b)<sup>x</sup>? {
= The repeated multiplication factor#Correct. The base b tells how the output changes for each one-unit increase in x.
~ The x-intercept#The base does not directly identify an x-intercept.
~ The starting output only#The starting output is a.
~ The exponent value only#The exponent is x, not b.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>If an exponential model begins at 30 and has base 1.5, what is the output at x=2? {
~ 45#This is the output at x = 1.
= 67.5#Correct. Compute 30 times 1.5 squared, which is 30 times 2.25.
~ 90#This treats two steps as multiplying by 3.
~ 33#This adds 1.5 twice.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which description matches f(x)=20(0.8)<sup>x</sup>? {
~ Starts at 0.8 and multiplies by 20.#This reverses the parameters.
~ Starts at 20 and adds 0.8 each step.#This treats the model as linear.
= Starts at 20 and multiplies by 0.8 each step.#Correct. The coefficient is 20 and the base is 0.8.
~ Starts at 16 and multiplies by 20.#16 is f(1), not the initial value.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>What type of change appears in 5, 15, 45, 135? {
~ Linear change#The differences are not constant.
~ No pattern#There is a clear repeated multiplication pattern.
~ Quadratic change#This sequence is identified by a common ratio, not second differences.
= Exponential change#Correct. The values multiply by 3 each step.
}

::ALG2_U04_L01_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which model represents an initial value of 1 and each step multiplying by 6? {
= f(x)=1(6)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. Initial value 1 and base 6 produce repeated multiplication by 6.
~ f(x)=6(1)<sup>x</sup>#This is constant at 6.
~ f(x)=1+6x#This is linear.
~ f(x)=6x#This is proportional, not exponential.
}

